WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.530
Two credential-shaped strings in the shipped bundle.

2
00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:09.580
Is this a leak? The names cannot tell you.

3
00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:13.870
Ask: what can each value actually do?

4
00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:21.840
A name is a label. A capability is what the upstream will perform.

5
00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:27.260
Public id as bearer → 403. It names a project; it authorises nothing.

6
00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:32.270
'Public' is a tested fact here, not a promise from the name.

7
00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:35.680
Synthetic secret → 200. The operation runs.

8
00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:39.580
Anyone who downloaded the bundle holds this capability.

9
00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:46.160
Fix: the backend holds the secret in its own config.

10
00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:54.720
A forwarding proxy is the leak with an extra hop: authenticate, authorise, validate.

11
00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.380
No session → 401. Viewer role → 403.

12
00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:02.290
Neither request reaches the upstream.

13
00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:09.410
Maintainer → 200. One upstream call, by the backend.

14
00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:17.320
No secret in body or headers. Over-broad operation → 400.

15
00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:22.450
Scan JS, HTML, config payloads, source maps if produced.

16
00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:30.440
0 secret-like, after the scanner proved it finds the planted one. Not zero everywhere.

17
00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:35.950
A public identifier names something. A secret grants capability.

18
00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:40.860
Vendor browser keys differ; CORS is not authorisation.

19
00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:47.600
A leaked production secret must be rotated. Removal revokes no copies.
